six members byte-identical; `:applications` 263/256 unchanged.\n22\t- **Honest-gate**: Jolt refusal recorded (not bypassed) — same four UNLISTED namespaces, zero children, correctly reported as J2-owner dependency.\n23\t- **Counts vs claims** all match: JVM 46/605 + 10/91,387, compiler 116/1,082; Node 41/516 + 10/91,387; Dart 41/10 all-pass; kondo 0 err/1 pre-existing warning; RED log 10 tests / 91,115 failures (genuine red-before-green).\n24\t- **SHA pins**: all 8 match delivered files. **No self-generated goldens.** **ANTLR**: `clojure -M:antlr-gen` is an equivalent launcher; compiler tests passed, and the Jolt refusal is manifest-based, not parser-availability-based.\n25\t\n26\t## Findings\n27\t\n28\t**F1 (blocking)** — Family-4 set-element tie skipped under a false \"unconstructable\" premise. `test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc:535-549` claims a whole-element set tie is unconstructable because \"every host set constructor collapses two NaNs\". This is contradicted by the *same file*: the map-tie block (`~516-527`) builds `(assoc {} ##NaN :left ##NaN (list '+ 'n 1))` and asserts `(= 2 (count pairs))` — \"both NaN entries survive\" — and that assertion is green on the JVM. Maps and sets share equality/hash semantics (`=` on doubles is `==` → `(= ##NaN ##NaN)` is false), so two `##NaN` survive in a set exactly as in a map. The block actually written (`#{inner (list '+ 'n 1)}` with `inner = (+ n 0)`) creates *no tie* (distinct contents). RULING 3 test 4 requires \"distinct forms compiling to an identical entire pair/element to exercise ties\" — the \"pair\" half is done, the \"element\" half is not, and the ruling says a missing required comparison is \"a failed/incomplete gate, not a green skip.\" **Disposition**: either add the set tie (`(hash-set ##NaN ##NaN)` → assert 2 element occurrences + equal `element-keys`), or provide REPL evidence per host that `(count #{##NaN ##NaN})` is 1.\n29\t\n30\t**F2 (minor)** — `ascending?` uses `(neg? (canonical-compare …))`, so it's strict and cannot certify a tie as sorted; author correctly asserts ties via explicit `=`, but the helper name over-promises.\n31\t\n32\t**F3 (minor)** — \"identical compiled set elements tie\" block title is misleading (no tie is constructed).\n33\t\n34\t## Conclusion\n35\t\n36\tEmitter + versioning + re-pin + bounds + honest-gate are correct and well-evidenced. Blocking solely on F1's disposition (family-4 set-element tie coverage under a likely-false boundary claim).\n37\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"8dfe7bc7-e9e3-4060-9095-ab96a889532e","timestamp":"2026-11-18T19:47:57.418Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795030110000-reviewer-fix-constructed-collection-order.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md","content":"I could not persist the findings file — the permission layer denied every write (Write tool ×3, Bash heredoc ×1) to `collab/yang-clojure/`. The deliverable below is complete and ready to be saved by the orchestrator at the mirror path.\n\n---\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-18 19:41:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-19 02:41:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n# Verdict: CHANGES-REQUIRED\n\n**Deliverable path (blocked from writing — please place here):**\n`collab/yang-clojure/1795030110000-reviewer-fix-constructed-collection-order.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md`\n\nOne blocking test-coverage finding (a likely-false \"boundary\" that skips a ruling-required tie comparison), plus two minor notes. Everything else — RULING 1 mechanics, RULING 2 scope, RULING 3 bounds, RULING 4 versioning, the re-pin, and the honest-gate — checks out.\n\n## What verified clean\n\n- **RULING 1 mechanics** (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:302-372`): single compile per child, `[key-root value-root]` sort via `jing/canonical-compare` over `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` roots (confirmed `jing.cljc:188` is the ruling's own unsigned bytewise CBOR comparator), seed first, no 