r different buckets... ugh, empirics!\n\nFINAL probe list (JVM):\na. (count (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]))                      ; r1: 2\nb. (count (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN 1]))                    ; ? replicate r1 collapse\nc. (count (conj (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]) 1))             ; ?\nd. (count (hash-set ##NaN ##NaN))\ne. (count (apply hash-set [##NaN ##NaN 1]))\nf. (count (set [##NaN ##NaN]))\ng. (count (conj #{} ##NaN ##NaN))\nh. (count (into (into #{} [##NaN]) [##NaN]))             ; mixed\ni. keys count of (assoc {} ##NaN 1 ##NaN 2)              ; sanity 2\n\nThen use whichever keeps 3 WITH a dynamic element. If none: use (a)-style for the NaN pair and conj the dynamic element (c). If (c) collapses → hmm — maybe build the SET as the emitter input via a set that CONTAINS the dynamic app FIRST: `(into #{(list '+ 'n 0)} [##NaN ##NaN])`?\n\nIf literally no JVM construction preserves both NaNs alongside a third element, then the fallback per the reviewer: REPL evidence per host that the count is 1 (boundary proof). But the reviewer's premise says it SHOULD work. Let me run the probe.\n\nAlso for the JVM→CLJS/CLJD portability: on those hosts NaN≠NaN in `=`/hash everywhere, so all constructions keep both — the host-conditional construction in the test can even be JVM-specific without hurting others.\n\nTHEN: F2 rename `ascending?` → `strictly-ascending?` (+docstring fix). Where is `ascending?` used? Many places (permutation loops assert every ctor strictly ascending — post-fix there are no ties in those corpora, all entries distinct → strict holds ✓ keep semantics). The arity-map oracle, colliding test, entry-points, oracle test, set/map corpus loops. Rename all occurrences.\n\nWait — careful: are there places where a tie CAN occur and the strict check would now be wrong? The permutation corpora use distinct entries → no ties → strict fine. The metadata cases: value-meta key `(with-meta (list '+ 'n 1) {:line 8})` vs `(list '+ 'n 2)` — distinct roots. The NaN pair-tie block: uses `ascending?`? Let me check the map-tie blocks in r1: \"the value root breaks equal key roots\" asserts `(is (ascending? pairs))` — pairs = [[nan :left] [nan app]] — pair KEYS differ (values differ) → strict holds ✓. \"identical compiled pairs tie\" block — pairs equal → did it assert ascending?? r1 code: `(is (= 2 (count pairs))) (is (= (first pairs) (second pairs))) (is (= 5 ...))` — no ascending ✓ good.\n\nNew set-tie block: assert element-keys equals its stable sort (non-decreasing) + first=second + operand count.\n\nF3: retitle. With F1 fixed the block genuinely constructs a tie → title stays accurate; but I'll keep the distinct-elements occurrence-preservation block separately with an ACCURATE title (\"compiled element occurrences survive…\"), which it already has in my r1 final? The r1 final block title WAS \"identical compiled set elements tie without occurrence loss\" over DISTINCT elements (the misleading one). New plan:\n- Block 1: \"identical compiled set elements tie without occurrence loss\" — the REAL NaN tie.\n- Block 2 (from r1's distinct-elements one): retitle \"every compiled element occurrence survives: distinct elements evaluating to one value keep their occurrences\" — wait, that one used `#{inner (list '+ 'n 1)}` with inner=(+ n 0) — distinct CONTENTS. The earlier \"colliding-value-expressions\" idea got dropped. The title should say what it constructs: two distinct dynamic elements (and the set-corpus already covers colliding values). Title: \"distinct dynamic elements keep their compiled occurrences\". Fine.\n\nAlso the r2 findings doc + update the FALSE boundary claim text in the test (replace with the actual result per F1: \"Replace the false boundary claim with the actual result\").\n\nThen gates: JVM ordering ns + callsite battery; Node + Dart lanes for the new namespace; kondo. Same command forms as r1 — I need the temp driver namespace again (deleted at r1 end). Recreate it (with only the needed gates: node-order, dart-order, kondo; jvm runs direct).\n\nPlan:\n1. Probe JVM NaN set constructions (temp diag namespace).\n2. Fix the test file (F1 block + F2 rename + F3 title + corrected boundary comment).\n3. JVM runs (ordering + callsite).\n4. Node + Dart lanes for ordering ns (driver).\n5. kondo (driver).\n6. r2 findings doc.\n7. Delete temp namespaces.\n8. Update memory.\n\nLet me start. First check worktree state (should be as r1 left it).","signature":"06bb9d003c0b45f8a87a07f5"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"1ceab857-3b6b-4701-8b65-a1de970b497e","timestamp":"2026-11-18T19:49:56.059Z","thinking_duration_ms":113162,"thinking_display":"updates"}
