{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202611190350136c3bb97152ee4822","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"DEFINITIVE JVM results:\n\n- `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN])` → **2** ✓ keeps\n- `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN 1])` → **3** ✓ KEEPS ALL THREE!\n- `(conj (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]) 1)` → 3 ✓\n- `(hash-set ##NaN ##NaN)` → **1** collapses (createWithCheck uses .equals)\n- `(apply hash-set [##NaN ##NaN 1])` → **3** ✓ keeps!\n- `(set [##NaN ##NaN])` → 2 ✓ keeps\n- `(conj #{} ##NaN ##NaN)` → 1 collapses (persistent node path)\n- `(assoc {} ##NaN 1 ##NaN 2)` → 2 ✓ (r1 known)\n\nSo `into` (transient path) KEEPS NaN duplicates even with a third element — `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN 1])` = 3. My r1 recollection of a collapse with `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0)])` was WRONG — the probe says 3 elements. What did the r1 failure actually show then? keys = [[88491ea0] [f9f89b43]] with count-2 assertion PASSING... wait — if the set had 3 elements {nan nan app}, then element-keys = [[nanroot] [nanroot] [approot]] — count = 3, but the assertion `(= 2 (count keys))` PASSED?? No wait — in the r1 run where `(= (first keys) (second keys))` failed with [88491ea0] vs [f9f89b43], the `(= 2 (count keys))` assertion at 522 was... let me re-look at that r1 output:\n\n```\nFAIL 516: (= 2 (count pairs)) → (not (= 2 1))     [map tie via into {} — collapsed]\nFAIL 517: (= (first pairs) (second pairs)) → second = nil\nFAIL 518: (= 5 (count (:operands ctor))) → (not (= 5 3))\nFAIL 522: ??? — not shown in that grep output\nFAIL 524: (= (first keys) (second keys)) → (not (= [88491ea0] [f9f89b43]))\n```\n\n522 `(= 2 (count keys))` — hmm wait, in THAT run the set form was `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0)])` and the count assertion was `(= 2 (count keys))` — WAIT that would FAIL with 3 elements! Unless... the r1 assertion list showed \"1 FAIL ... 522\" — hmm the grep in that round showed `1 FAIL in (order-pairing...) (522)` in one run and 524