ble.NaN)` = `(hash 0.0)`? NO — Clojure's `Numbers.hasheq(double d)`: `return new Double(d).hashCode();` → NaN hash = 1910056592 (0x7FF8000000000000 bits based — same for both NaNs ✓ same bucket).\n\nHmm, what about `Util.equiv(Object k1, Object k2)`:\n```java\nstatic public boolean equiv(Object k1, Object k2){\n\tif(k1 == k2)\n\t\treturn true;\n\tif(k1 != null) {\n\t\tif(k1 instanceof Number && k2 instanceof Number)  // ??? \n```\nHmm no — actually in Clojure's Util.java:\n```java\nstatic public boolean equiv(Object k1, Object k2){\n\tif(k1 == k2)\n\t\treturn true;\n\tif(k1 != null)\n\t\treturn k1.equals(k2);   // ← for the generic Object path??\n\treturn false;\n}\n```\nNO — that's `equals`-based. But `=` (the var) is `clojure.core/=` → `Util.equiv` with Number dispatch:\n```java\nstatic public boolean equiv(Object k1, Object k2){\n\tif(k1 == k2)\n\t\treturn true;\n\tif(k1 != null) {\n\t\tif(k1 instanceof Number && k2 instanceof Number)\n\t\t\treturn Numbers.equal((Number) k1, (Number) k2);\n\t\telse if(k1 instanceof IPersistentCollection ...)\n\t}\n\treturn false / k1.equals(k2);\n}\n```\nI genuinely don't remember. BUT the empirical facts:\n- map: `(assoc {} ##NaN 1 ##NaN 2)` → 2 (PersistentArrayMap.assoc — linear, keeps). \n- map: `(hash-map ##NaN :l ##NaN :r)` → 1 (createWithCheck — collapses).\n- map: `(into {} [[##NaN v][##NaN v]])` → collapsed in r1 (transient → collapses!).\n- set: `(into #{} [nan nan])` → 2 keeps; `(into #{} [nan nan 1])` → 3 keeps; `(into #{} [nan nan app])` → 2 LOSES ONE.\n\nThe map-into collapsed but set-into kept?! And set-into keeps with a number third element but loses with a list third element!! This is genuinely bizarre. Unless... wait — could the READER be evaluating `##NaN` inside `[##NaN ##NaN (list ...)]` differently? No.\n\nHmm, hold on — let me reconsider: `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN])` — how does `into` build? `(reduce conj! (transient #{}) coll)` — wait, actually `into` with no xform: `(clojure.core/reduce conj (if (instance? clojure.lang.IEditableCollection to) (persistent! (reduce conj! (transient to) from)) to) ...)` — for sets: transient path with conj!.\n\nTransientHashMap conj! of nan: `doAssoc(key, val)` → `root.assoc(leafFlag...)`. The BitmapIndexedNode.assoc(edit, shift, hash, key, val, leafFlag) — when a duplicate hash is found: `if(node.key != null && node.key == key)`?? Some paths use `==` (REFERENCE equality)! `Util.equiv` vs reference `==`. In the ARRAY-NODE/BITMAP path with a hash collision, the code does:\n```java\nif(equiv(n.key, key)) { ...replace... } else { ...create CollisionNode... }\n```\nHmm — if it used `equiv` → Numbers.equal → false → COLLISION NODE holding BOTH NaNs → both kept (consistent with [nan nan] → 2 and [nan nan 1] → 3!).\n\nThen why does [nan nan app] lose one?! ... \n\nWAIT. Maybe I should check: does it lose the NaN or the APP? nan-count 1, host-count 2 → one NaN + the app. Hmm what if... the APP's hash COLLIDES with NaN's hash?! `(hash (list '+ 'n 0))` — could equal 1910056592?? Astronomically unlikely but... the hash of `(list '+ 'n 0)` is computed from its elements. If by chance it equals the NaN hash bucket... 1/2^32 chance. Unlikely.\n\nDifferent theory — THE ORDER OF CONJ: `(into #{} [a b c])` conjs nan, nan, app. Result {nan, app}... \n\nHmm, let me look from a different angle: maybe the READER of the VECTOR `[##NaN ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0)]` — the two ##NaN literals — vectors don't dedup. But wait — is the second ##NaN in the SOURCE actually read as a separate object? Yes.\n\nYou know what — empiricism beats theory. Try alternative constructions with the app:\n1. `(into #{} (list ##NaN ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0)))` \n2. `(into #{} [##NaN (list '+ 'n 0) ##NaN])` — app in the middle\n3. `(conj (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]) (list '+ 'n 0))` — probe c earlier with `1` → 3; with app?\n4. `(into (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]) [(list '+ 'n 0)])`\n5. `(into #{} [(list '+ 'n 0) ##NaN ##NaN])` — app first\n6. Does the app's POSITION in the vector matter? With [1] third → 3 kept. With [1] MIDDLE: `(into #{} [##NaN 1 ##NaN])`?\n7. Also test with a STRING third: `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN \"x\"])`.\n8. And a keyword third.\n\nThe pattern might be: the transient's in-place edit keeps the 2nd NaN when the node is small; adding certain elements triggers a `persistent!` re-... no, persistent! happens once at the end.\n\nOHHH WAIT. I think I might have it: `(list '+ 'n 0)` in my probe/test source — how is `'(list ...)` being read?! In the probe I wrote `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0)])` — this is CODE: `(list '+ 'n 0)` evaluates to a seq `(+ n 0)`. Fine. Hmm.\n\nLet me just run the alternatives probe.","signature":"71a1fca325274d46abeeaec3"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"b5d4478b-3f64-4ed1-b15e-1c450e81dd0b","timestamp":"2026-11-18T19:57:58.183Z","thinking_duration_ms":25625,"thinking_display":"updates"}
