sient-built alone, then add the app. The \"why\" might be: [nan, nan] transient session → HashCollisionNode with 2 entries (same hash) → persistent! → then a NORMAL persistent conj of app → kept. While [nan nan app] in ONE session → BitmapIndexedNode.edit path... The mechanism difference doesn't matter for the test as long as the construction is deterministic. But I SHOULD understand enough to trust it — let me run (d) and (e) with several different dynamic elements and orders to confirm stability, plus one more probe: [nan, nan, app, app2] etc. Also — SANITY: maybe test `(into #{} [##NaN ##NaN (list 1)])` (a cons of one number).\n\nAnd one more thought — r1's ORIGINAL confusion is now fully explained: everything I observed was real, just never isolated the seq-third-element trigger. The boundary claim was wrong for a subtler reason than the reviewer's exact argument (their map/set symmetry claim is also imprecise — `into` on a MAP collapsed in r1 while `into` on a 2-SET kept — but their bottom line stands: a constructable tie exists).","signature":"83db32f300074412aeaa8a75"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"66fbe358-a5ad-4cfb-b102-f61a3ea088ee","timestamp":"2026-11-18T19:59:54.113Z","thinking_duration_ms":10869,"thinking_display":"updates"}
