{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261119035959a68e4c63103b4ffd","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_12cdb32207474c0a9b294889","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc\")\nt = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"    ;; two NaN elements are host-unequal (a NaN is never = to itself) and\n    ;; compile to one quiet-NaN row, so they are the whole-element tie.\n    ;; Which host constructions retain them is per-constructor, not a map\n    ;; vs set difference: the reduce/transient builders (into, and apply\n    ;; hash-set over a built sequence) keep both NaNs -- the JVM probe\n    ;; recorded in the r2 findings counts (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN 1]) at 3\n    ;; -- while the duplicate-checking constructors (the hash-set function\n    ;; and chained persistent conj) collapse them under Java equals. The\n    ;; dynamic third element is what keeps the set off the constant fast\n    ;; path so it reaches the emitter at all.\n    (let [nan-key [(root-of (yang/compile-form ##NaN false {}))]\n          form (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0)])\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"    ;; two NaN elements are host-unequal (a NaN is never = to itself) and\n    ;; compile to one quiet-NaN row, so they are the whole-element tie.\n    ;; Retention is per constructor, not a map-vs-set difference: on the\n    ;; JVM, into over exactly the NaN pair keeps both (a persistent conj\n    ;; chain collapses them under Java equals, and one transient session\n    ;; that also mixes in a seq element loses one -- the r2 findings\n    ;; record the probe), so the pair is built and finalized before the\n    ;; dynamic element joins. That element is what keeps the set off the\n    ;; constant fast path so it reaches the emitter at all. On Node and\n    ;; Dart no constructor collapses the pair (= is === / Dart ==, and\n    ;; NaN never equals itself there).\n    (let [nan-key [(root-of (yang/compile-form ##NaN false {}))]\n          form (conj (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN]) (list '+ 'n 0))\"\"\"\nassert old in t; t = t.replace(old, new)\np.write_text(t)\nprint(\"tie construction fixed\")\nPYEOF\nclojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-collection-order-test 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"Use two-stage tie construction and run JVM","timeout":900000}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"0563367d-ea31-4ae6-82ec-2ca73f190b73","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:00:15.935Z","wire_tool_inputs":{"call_12cdb32207474c0a9b294889":{"command":"python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc\")\nt = p.read_text()\nold = \"\"\"    ;; two NaN elements are host-unequal (a NaN is never = to itself) and\n    ;; compile to one quiet-NaN row, so they are the whole-element tie.\n    ;; Which host constructions retain them is per-constructor, not a map\n    ;; vs set difference: the reduce/transient builders (into, and apply\n    ;; hash-set over a built sequence) keep both NaNs -- the JVM probe\n    ;; recorded in the r2 findings counts (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN 1]) at 3\n    ;; -- while the duplicate-checking constructors (the hash-set function\n    ;; and chained persistent conj) collapse them under Java equals. The\n    ;; dynamic third element is what keeps the set off the constant fast\n    ;; path so it reaches the emitter at all.\n    (let [nan-key [(root-of (yang/compile-form ##NaN false {}))]\n          form (into #{} [##NaN ##NaN (list '+ 'n 0)])\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"    ;; two NaN elements are host-unequal (a NaN is never = to itself) and\n    ;; compile to one quiet-NaN row, so they are the whole-element tie.\n    ;; Retention is per constructor, not a map-vs-set difference: on the\n    ;; JVM, into over exactly the NaN pair keeps both (a persistent 