{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261119040503032f022c34ae45a8","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"\"Unsupported escape character: \\.\" — the driver file's CFG_ORDER replacement produced `\\\"^yang\\.clojure...\"` — a single backslash before `.` inside a Clojure string → invalid escape. In r1 the file ended with `\\\\\\\\.` (two chars in the file = `\\\\` escape = runtime `\\`... wait — r1's final driver had `\"^yang\\\\\\\\.clojure...\"`? Let me check what r1 had: the grep showed `\"{:ns-regexp \\\\\\\"^yang\\\\\\\\.clojure-callsite-test$\\\\\\\"}\"` — file contains `\\\\.` (2 backslashes) → runtime string contains `\\.` — but WAIT, that FAILED in shadow's EDN parse (\"Unsupported escape character: \\.\") and I quadrupled to `\\\\\\\\.` (4 in file → 2 at runtime → EDN escape `\\\\` = one backslash ✓). So the runtime arg needs TWO literal backslashes → the Clojure file string needs FOUR. My python: `ns_order = \"yang\" + B + \".clojure-...\"` (1 backslash) → cfg has 1 → file escape invalid. Need `B + B` in the runtime string → `B*4` in file. Fix: ns = \"yang\" + B + B + \".clojure-...\".","signature":"e40464fd1e6b42be82bdaa9a"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"2432eb64-f0ab-4127-a5c7-b1824e136779","timestamp":"2026-11-18T20:05:10.865Z","thinking_duration_ms":2973,"thinking_display":"updates"}
